title: 'Pre-flight established for the first real Warpline project: nine blockers, three from measurement rather than review'
summary: |
  Three parallel agents — storage measurement, retention/durability design, and observability design — produced a consolidated pre-flight for committing a real project to Warpline. The most valuable output was not a design but a demonstration: `hook install` reports success, `git commit` exits 0, no fabric is ever created, and `warpline status` says "clean". Storage measurement overturned the retention question entirely (pruning would reclaim 12% while 96.5% is redundancy), and surfaced a hard ceiling nobody knew about: Warpline cannot seal a repo above ~100k symbols. Two agents contradicted each other on whether contentId is a safe dedup key; the reconciliation is what makes the 19.6x storage fix legal.</summary>
  <body>## The demonstration that reordered the list

  Helix ran this in a scratch repo and it is the whole problem in one transcript:

  ```
  $ warpline hook install     → "auto-seal created"
  $ git commit                → exit 0
  $ ls .warpline              → No such file or directory
  $ warpline status           → "clean — no semantic change", exit 0
  ```

  Nothing was sealed. No fabric was ever created. Every surface reported success. The trigger is that `warpline` is not on PATH — the default for a local `npm i`, and verified true in this repo, which survives only on the `dist/cli.js` fallback at hook.ts:41. A positive control with WARPLINE_BIN set sealed correctly, so the mechanism works and only its failure is invisible.

  That is a rocket flying with an empty fabric and every green light on, and it moved the hook fix to the top of the list ahead of every design item.

  ## Measurement beat design on the storage question

  Arky's ruling was careful and well-argued: reclaim losslessly, do not buy a gc, do not spend the "nothing is ever deleted" property that makes sealed work outlast a git reflog. Correct conclusion. But the measurement pass showed the property was never at risk, for a reason no amount of design reasoning would have produced:

  - `states/` is a CACHE. The code says so at store.ts:53, and restore.ts / materialize.ts / object-store.ts contain zero references to it. Restore reconstructs from the 35 MB native object store.
  - Per-seal cost is the FULL symbol graph re-serialized, always — linear in object count, provably independent of changed files (a 1-file and a 20-file seal produced identical deltas).
  - Only 12% of states/ is unreachable garbage. 96.5% is redundancy: 468,355 object instances storing 13,848 unique, 29x.

  So a retention policy — the thing that was about to be designed — would have reclaimed 123 MB and left the 29x untouched. The lever was never pruning.

  ## The contradiction that had to be resolved rather than averaged

  Arky (F-B): contentId is not a usable dedup key — 57% of keys map to more than one distinct body; keying on it would be a wrong-merge generator.

  Measurement: codeEssence is 100% invariant per contentId — 42,819 instances, zero exceptions.

  Both true, measuring different things. The 57% variation lives in carried provenance (description, tags, anchors, codeSignature); the codeEssence payload never varies. Arky's warning stands against keying the whole body; the narrower move of externalizing only codeEssence is safe. Without explicitly reconciling these, F-B would have blocked the 19.6x fix on a true-but-misapplied objection — an averaged or deferred answer would have been strictly worse than either agent's.

  ## The instrument already exists and is wired to the wrong path

  oracle.ts already computes the meaning-vs-git confusion matrix (agreeClean / agreeConflict / divergeGitOnly / divergeMeaningOnly). The git counterfactual is mergeTree() at git-exec.ts:544. Both work. But oracle is an offline manual verb, shadow.ts never calls mergeTree, and oracle.jsonl has zero readers — 11 MB, 28 rows, written and never once folded.

  So the one item that is unanswerable retroactively is not a build. It is connecting two existing pieces to the live verdict row.

  ## New hard ceiling

  Warpline cannot seal above ~100k symbols: 100,008 objects seals, 125,008 fails with Maximum call stack size exceeded. Object-count driven, reproduced at two different file counts, site is essenceOf at essence-hash.ts:372 — the one remaining recursive graph walk. This directly constrains which project can be the test, at roughly 4,000 files.

  ## The list

  Nine blocking items: un-silence the hook (~6 lines), fix the state-cache ENOSPC swallow, un-ignore refs/heads (1 line), gzip states (1 call site, 7.5x), the counterfactual field on the shadow row, `warpline health`, confirm C-2/C-3/C-5 on the run branch, confirm the project gets its own root, and raise the symbol ceiling if the project is large. Four one-liners plus the hook fix are half the risk.

  Machine state at the time of writing: 7.6 GiB free, 99% full, down from 9.3 GiB during the session.</body>
